t}. Debounced: {debouncedCount}.</p>\n<p>Pending: {debouncer.state.isPending}</p>\n<button onclick={() => debouncer.flush()}>Flush</button>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"child-subscriptions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Child subscriptions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"utility.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" with a child snippet to select state without updating the utility owner's selection. Removing the component releases its subscription."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<debouncer.Subscribe selector={(state) => ({ isPending: state.isPending })}>\n  {#snippet children({ isPending })}\n    <span>Pending: {isPending}</span>\n  {/snippet}\n</debouncer.Subscribe>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reactive-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reactive options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass a plain options object, property getters, or an options factory. The adapter evaluates top-level getters, while function-valued core options remain callbacks. Options update the existing utility; pending work, counters, and the store retain their identity."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Options retain the core partial-merge behavior. Omitting a key preserves the previous setting; explicitly passing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" clears it. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialItems"},{"type":"text","value":" initialize the utility once and do not recreate it on later updates."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"default-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wrap descendants in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PacerProvider"},{"type":"text","value":", or call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"providePacerOptions(() => defaults)"},{"type":"text","value":" during component initialization. Group defaults by utility, such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ debouncer: { leading: true } }"},{"type":"text","value":". Local options take precedence."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"cleanup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cleanup"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Debouncers, throttlers, and batchers cancel pending timers by default. Queuers stop processing. Async variants also abort active work. Synchronous rate limiters need no timer cleanup."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUnmount"},{"type":"text","value":" to replace the default cleanup, for example to call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"flush()"},{"type":"text","value":" before leaving a page. The callback receives the adapter instance and its selected state. If you replace cleanup for an async utility, call its cancellation or abort methods when needed."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"event-handlers-and-value-helpers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Event handlers and value helpers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use an instance method as the event handler: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maybeExecute"},{"type":"text","value":" for debouncing, throttling, and rate limiting, or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"addItem"},{"type":"text","value":" for batching. The instance also provides control methods and state subscriptions."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State helpers return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[value, setValue, utility]"},{"type":"text","value":"; value helpers return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[value, utility]"},{"type":"text","value":". Read values by calling their accessors. Setters accept a new value or a functional update. Synchronous queue state helpers return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[itemsAccessor, addItem, utility]"},{"type":"text","value":". Async queue state helpers return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[itemsAccessor, utility]"},{"type":"text","value":"; call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"utility.addItem()"},{"type":"text","value":" to enqueue an item. Queued value helpers return the last processed value, rather than the list of pending items."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"async-utilities","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Async utilities"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The five async utilities preserve typed results and core error behavior. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSuccess"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSettled"},{"type":"text","value":" for outcomes, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"asyncRetryerOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" for retry configuration. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"abort()"},{"type":"text","value":" is cooperative: your operation must observe the supplied abort signal. See the individual async guides for scheduling and concurrency details."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"devtools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Devtools"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Install "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/svelte-pacer-devtools"},{"type":"text","value":" and use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pacerDevtoolsPlugin"},{"type":"text","value":" with the framework TanStack Devtools integration. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/production"},{"type":"text","value":" entry explicitly includes the panel in production builds."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"api-reference","children":[{"type":"text","value":"API reference"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./reference/index.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"generated reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for signatures, options, and return types."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"installation","text":"Installation","level":2},{"id":"lifecycle-and-state","text":"Lifecycle and state","level":2},{"id":"api-overview","text":"API overview","level":2},{"id":"example","text":"Example","level":2},{"id":"child-subscriptions","text":"Child subscriptions","level":2},{"id":"reactive-options","text":"Reactive options","level":2},{"id":"default-options","text":"Default options","level":2},{"id":"cleanup","text":"Cleanup","level":2},{"id":"event-handlers-and-value-helpers","text":"Event handlers and value helpers","level":2},{"id":"async-utilities","text":"Async utilities","level":2},{"id":"devtools","text":"Devtools","level":2},{"id":"api-reference","text":"API reference","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nThe `svelte-pacer` adapter connects Pacer scheduling utilities to Svelte state and lifecycle management. It re-exports the core package, including utility classes, stateless functions, option types, and async retrying.\n\n## Installation\n\n```sh\npnpm add @tanstack/svelte-pacer\n```\n\nThe package is ESM-only and requires Node.js 20 or newer when running in Node.js.\n\n## Lifecycle and state\n\nCall factories during component initialization. Options update in a pre-render effect. Read selected state through `utility.state` without destructuring it outside a reactive expression. Component destruction releases subscriptions and cleans up the utility.\n\nBy default, the selected state is `{}`. Pass a selector to subscribe only to the fields your UI reads. The underlying `store` remains available for additional subscriptions.\n\n## API overview\n\n| Utility | Instance API | State and value helpers |\n| ------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| [batching](./guides/batching.md) | `createBatcher` | None |\n| [debouncing](./guides/debouncing.md) | `createDebouncer` | `createDebouncedSignal`, `createDebouncedValue` |\n| [queuing](./guides/queuing.md) | `createQueuer` | `createQueuedSignal`, `createQueuedValue` |\n| [rate limiting](./guides/rate-limiting.md) | `createRateLimiter` | `createRateLimitedSignal`, `createRateLimitedValue` |\n| [throttling](./guides/throttling.md) | `createThrottler` | `createThrottledSignal`, `createThrottledValue` |\n| [async batching](./guides/async-batching.md) | `createAsyncBatcher` | None |\n| [async debouncing](./guides/async-debouncing.md) | `createAsyncDebouncer` | None |\n| [async queuing](./guides/async-queuing.md) | `createAsyncQueuer` | `createAsyncQueuedSignal` |\n| [async rate limiting](./guides/async-rate-limiting.md) | `createAsyncRateLimiter` | None |\n| [async throttling](./guides/async-throttling.md) | `createAsyncThrottler` | None |\n\n## Example\n\nThis counter coalesces rapid clicks into one update after 500 ms. Flush applies the latest pending count immediately.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createDebouncer } from '@tanstack/svelte-pacer'\n\n  let count = $state(0)\n  let debouncedCount = $state(0)\n  const debouncer = createDebouncer(\n    (value: number) => {\n      debouncedCount = value\n    },\n    { wait: 500 },\n    (state) => ({ isPending: state.isPending }),\n  )\n  function increment() {\n    debouncer.maybeExecute(++count)\n  }\n</script>\n\n<button onclick={increment}>Increment</button>\n<p>Count: {count}. Debounced: {debouncedCount}.</p>\n<p>Pending: {debouncer.state.isPending}</p>\n<button onclick={() => debouncer.flush()}>Flush</button>\n```\n\n## Child subscriptions\n\nUse `utility.Subscribe` with a child snippet to select state without updating the utility owner's selection. Removing the component releases its subscription.\n\n```svelte\n<debouncer.Subscribe selector={(state) => ({ isPending: state.isPending })}>\n  {#snippet children({ isPending })}\n    <span>Pending: {isPending}</span>\n  {/snippet}\n</debouncer.Subscribe>\n```\n\n## Reactive options\n\nPass a plain options object, property getters, or an options factory. The adapter evaluates top-level getters, while function-valued core options remain callbacks. Options update the existing utility; pending work, counters, and the store retain their identity.\n\nOptions retain the core partial-merge behavior. Omitting a key preserves the previous setting; explicitly passing `undefined` clears it. `key`, `initialState`, and `initialItems` initialize the utility once and do not recreate it on later updates.\n\n## Default options\n\nWrap descendants in `PacerProvider`, or call `providePacerOptions(() => defaults)` during component initialization. Group defaults by utility, such as `{ debouncer: { leading: true } }`. Local options take precedence.\n\n## Cleanup\n\nDebouncers, throttlers, and batchers cancel pending timers by default. Queuers stop processing. Async variants also abort active work. Synchronous rate limiters need no timer cleanup.\n\nSet `onUnmount` to replace the default cleanup, for example to call `flush()` before leaving a page. The callback receives the adapter instance and its selected state. If you replace cleanup for an async utility, call its cancellation or abort methods when needed.\n\n## Event handlers and value helpers\n\nUse an instance method as the event handler: `maybeExecute` for debouncing, throttling, and rate limiting, or `addItem` for batching. The instance also provides control methods and state subscriptions.\n\nState helpers return `[value, setValue, utility]`; value helpers return `[value, utility]`. Read values by calling their accessors. Setters accept a new value or a functional update. Synchronous queue state helpers return `[itemsAccessor, addItem, utility]`. Async queue state helpers return `[itemsAccessor, utility]`; call `utility.addItem()` to enqueue an item. Queued value helpers return the last processed value, rather than the list of pending items.\n\n## Async utilities\n\nThe five async utilities preserve typed results and core error behavior. Use `onSuccess`, `onError`, and `onSettled` for outcomes, and `asyncRetryerOptions` for retry configuration. `abort()` is cooperative: your operation must observe the supplied abort signal. See the individual async guides for scheduling and concurrency details.\n\n## Devtools\n\nInstall `@tanstack/svelte-pacer-devtools` and use `pacerDevtoolsPlugin` with the framework TanStack Devtools integration. The `/production` entry explicitly includes the panel in production builds.\n\n## API reference\n\nSee the [generated reference](./reference/index.md) for signatures, options, and return types.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Svelte Adapter\nid: adapter\n---\n\nThe `svelte-pacer` adapter connects Pacer scheduling utilities to Svelte state and lifecycle management. It re-exports the core package, including utility classes, stateless functions, option types, and async retrying.\n\n## Installation\n\n```sh\npnpm add @tanstack/svelte-pacer\n```\n\nThe package is ESM-only and requires Node.js 20 or newer when running in Node.js.\n\n## Lifecycle and state\n\nCall factories during component initialization. Options update in a pre-render effect. Read selected state through `utility.state` without destructuring it outside a reactive expression. Component destruction releases subscriptions and cleans up the utility.\n\nBy default, the selected state is `{}`. Pass a selector to subscribe only to the fields your UI reads. The underlying `store` remains available for additional subscriptions.\n\n## API overview\n\n| Utility | Instance API | State and value helpers |\n| ------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| [batching](./guides/batching.md) | `createBatcher` | None |\n| [debouncing](./guides/debouncing.md) | `createDebouncer` | `createDebouncedSignal`, `createDebouncedValue` |\n| [queuing](./guides/queuing.md) | `createQueuer` | `createQueuedSignal`, `createQueuedValue` |\n| [rate limiting](./guides/rate-limiting.md) | `createRateLimiter` | `createRateLimitedSignal`, `createRateLimitedValue` |\n| [throttling](./guides/throttling.md) | `createThrottler` | `createThrottledSignal`, `createThrottledValue` |\n| [async batching](./guides/async-batching.md) | `createAsyncBatcher` | None |\n| [async debouncing](./guides/async-debouncing.md) | `createAsyncDebouncer` | None |\n| [async queuing](./guides/async-queuing.md) | `createAsyncQueuer` | `createAsyncQueuedSignal` |\n| [async rate limiting](./guides/async-rate-limiting.md) | `createAsyncRateLimiter` | None |\n| [async throttling](./guides/async-throttling.md) | `createAsyncThrottler` | None |\n\n## Example\n\nThis counter coalesces rapid clicks into one update after 500 ms. Flush applies the latest pending count immediately.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createDebouncer } from '@tanstack/svelte-pacer'\n\n  let count = $state(0)\n  let debouncedCount = $state(0)\n  const debouncer = createDebouncer(\n    (value: number) => {\n      debouncedCount = value\n    },\n    { wait: 500 },\n    (state) => ({ isPending: state.isPending }),\n  )\n  function increment() {\n    debouncer.maybeExecute(++count)\n  }\n</script>\n\n<button onclick={increment}>Increment</button>\n<p>Count: {count}. Debounced: {debouncedCount}.</p>\n<p>Pending: {debouncer.state.isPending}</p>\n<button onclick={() => debouncer.flush()}>Flush</button>\n```\n\n## Child subscriptions\n\nUse `utility.Subscribe` with a child snippet to select state without updating the utility owner's selection. Removing the component releases its subscription.\n\n```svelte\n<debouncer.Subscribe selector={(state) => ({ isPending: state.isPending })}>\n  {#snippet children({ isPending })}\n    <span>Pending: {isPending}</span>\n  {/snippet}\n</debouncer.Subscribe>\n```\n\n## Reactive options\n\nPass a plain options object, property getters, or an options factory. The adapter evaluates top-level getters, while function-valued core options remain callbacks. Options update the existing utility; pending work, counters, and the store retain their identity.\n\nOptions retain the core partial-merge behavior. Omitting a key preserves the previous setting; explicitly passing `undefined` clears it. `key`, `initialState`, and `initialItems` initialize the utility once and do not recreate it on later updates.\n\n## Default options\n\nWrap descendants in `PacerProvider`, or call `providePacerOptions(() => defaults)` during component initialization. Group defaults by utility, such as `{ debouncer: { leading: true } }`. Local options take precedence.\n\n## Cleanup\n\nDebouncers, throttlers, and batchers cancel pending timers by default. Queuers stop processing. Async variants also abort active work. Synchronous rate limiters need no timer cleanup.\n\nSet `onUnmount` to replace the default cleanup, for example to call `flush()` before leaving a page. The callback receives the adapter instance and its selected state. If you replace cleanup for an async utility, call its cancellation or abort methods when needed.\n\n## Event handlers and value helpers\n\nUse an instance method as the event handler: `maybeExecute` for debouncing, throttling, and rate limiting, or `addItem` for batching. The instance also provides control methods and state subscriptions.\n\nState helpers return `[value, setValue, utility]`; value helpers return `[value, utility]`. Read values by calling their accessors. Setters accept a new value or a functional update. Synchronous queue state helpers return `[itemsAccessor, addItem, utility]`. Async queue state helpers return `[itemsAccessor, utility]`; call `utility.addItem()` to enqueue an item. Queued value helpers return the last processed value, rather than the list of pending items.\n\n## Async utilities\n\nThe five async utilities preserve typed results and core error behavior. Use `onSuccess`, `onError`, and `onSettled` for outcomes, and `asyncRetryerOptions` for retry configuration. `abort()` is cooperative: your operation must observe the supplied abort signal. See the individual async guides for scheduling and concurrency details.\n\n## Devtools\n\nInstall `@tanstack/svelte-pacer-devtools` and use `pacerDevtoolsPlugin` with the framework TanStack Devtools integration. The `/production` entry explicitly includes the panel in production builds.\n\n## API reference\n\nSee the [generated reference](./reference/index.md) for signatures, options, and return types.\n"}
